WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.290
- [Instructor] What we're
gonna do in this video

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.350
is do a few examples ordering numbers

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:04.970
that involve decimals.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.280
So let's say that we have the numbers

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.410
1.001, 0.113, and 1.101.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.460
What I would like you to do is order

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.250
these numbers from least to greatest.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.430
Take out some paper and try to do it

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.030
on your own before we do it together.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.590
All right, now let's do it together.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.290
And the way I would
tackle ordering numbers

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.160
is I would go to the largest place value

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.910
that the numbers have in common.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.460
And so, in this situation,

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.290
we have a ones place value in all of them.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:44.250
And so we can see that this has one one,

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.040
this has zero ones, and
this one has one one.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.070
So the thing that has the least ones

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.850
is going to be the
smallest of the numbers.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.180
So this one over here is going to be

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.300
the smallest of the numbers.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.490
So let me just write that over here.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.083
0.113.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.340
And now we have to figure
out which one is next

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.380
between 1.001 and 1.101.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.470
Well then we just go to
the next place value.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.940
So we go to the tenths place

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.130
and we right over here,

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.740
they're equal on the ones place.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.810
So if you go to the tenths place,

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:20.160
this one has zero tenths
while this one has one tenth.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.470
So the number on the right here

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.210
is going to be larger.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.570
It has more tenths.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.030
Same number of ones,
but it has more tenths.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.510
It doesn't really matter what happens

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:29.690
to the right of that.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.630
So the next smallest number,

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:33.580
if we're ordering from least to greatest,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.573
is going to be 1.001.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.130
And then last, but not
least, would be this one.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.810
That is the largest, 1.101.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.330
Let's do another example.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:51.403
Let's say we had the numbers 0.424,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.737
0.343, and 0.443.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.170
Pause this video and try to order these

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.420
from least to greatest on your own.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.950
And once again, the idea here is always

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.890
start with the largest place
value and then compare,

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:10.090
and then keep moving to the right

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.120
if some things are equal.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.410
All right, now let's do this together.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.180
So they all have zero ones,

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.300
so they're all equal there,

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.870
so that's not gonna tell us much.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.990
So now let's go to the tenths place.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.290
So here I have four tenths.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.480
Here I have three tenths.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.560
Here I have four tenths.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.460
So I don't even have to
look at the hundredths

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.200
or the thousandths place.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.010
This one has the least tenths.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.690
So I can put that as the least,

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.860
or the smallest of the
three numbers, 0.343

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.170
And now, so I've already used that one,

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:45.760
and so I need to compare
these two numbers.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.050
They have the same number of ones,

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.000
they have the same number of tenths,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.150
so then we move to the hundredths.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.640
So here I have two hundredths,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.120
here I have four hundredths.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.370
This one has less
hundredths than this one.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.300
So the one on the left is going to be

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.680
the next smallest number.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.163
So then we have 0.424.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:09.650
And then, last but not least,
this one right over here,

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.740
it has the same number of
ones as everything else.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.630
It has more tenths than this middle one,

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.400
and the same number of
tenths as the left one.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.820
But then it has, but then it also has

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.020
more hundredths than the
left one right over here.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.683
So this is the largest
of the numbers, 0.443.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:29.763
And we're done.

